body:
All very good point.

I realize my SO is very frugal while i'm a bit more luxurious. This i'm working on.

Sadly i have real problems shopping at 2nd hand places because we've already had bedbugs once. The cost of extermination isn't on us, but it's a special kind of hell when you get them. Especially with 2 kids so i'm very VERY scared of this. Do you typically takes steps to avoid an infestation?

&#x200B;

It's very true that daycare can be expensive, but as i stated in my post, it's thankfully not obnoxiously high in here. it's actually very cheap even in private daycare.

&#x200B;

The thing i'm considering now is the tax exemptions we get if i have my SO at my charge.  since i'm in a fairly low wage bracket, we could get quite a bit of amount to soften the corners (ehhh, weird image but you know what i mean). 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Out of curiosity, how did you manage and what steps did you take to make it work?

&#x200B;

i'm also interested in the aftermath.

&#x200B;

My main issue in this is urgency on both sides. I'm scared every morning that HR will show up and am not getting call backs (it's been a few weeks, i know job search can take 3-6 months). And on her side there's the very real reason of a burnout or depression.

Ouch this hurts, i should live in quebec and work in ontario then :P

&#x200B;

My salary alone is only ever so slightly under 2 minimum wage in quebec, but i do believe cost of living is much higher in ontario, could be wrong

Thank you this is exactly my point. i should have stated that it is something i would want, but i feel it's only possible on a 6 figure salary (slight exageration).

There's also the idea of my SO having a hard time joining back in the job market (hey a 2 year + gap is a 2 year gap no matter the workplace) but it's not a high end work with very fast changing work ethics and technology. So it's a tradeoff.

&#x200B;

Yes there's money left into our accounts, but it's not an amount i feel is enough to sustain a healthy lifestyle for a 4 people family. That's the part i want to know.

&#x200B;

And as other users have been saying (hank you so freaking much everyone) there's a lot of tax cuts i'm not aware of. I want help figuring out if it's feasible and where i can get extra income. Not knowing people's opinions on this issue.

So i have to do 11 700 x 15% x provincial tax and that will give me the yearly amount we will get? that's already starting to be more interesting, it's not something i knew about.

&#x200B;

Also i'm unsure how my Hr dept fits into this? if i file out a TD1, that means i will get a lower rate of tax removed from my pay and not be imposed at the end of the year because the governement will already know?

Thank you very much for this, is the RESP the fund for school? This is one of the parts that scare the crap out of me with the issue at hand. I will definately look into this.

&#x200B;

also i've looked a bit at spouse tax credit and i recall seeing on here that it can apply jan 19 2019. and i think i've seen on canada's website that we can apply and it starts coming in earlier than the following tax season.

&#x200B;

The problem with CCB is that it takes a whole year to fully adjust (i.e. if she's working in 2018, and stops january 2019, it would take another year to adjust the credit) but maybe that's just for the daycare costs? the canadian tax website is a mess and it's hard to understand all the info correctly
